### FSD Layer Rules

1. **Upper layers can only reference lower layers** (unidirectional dependency)

   - **Next.js**: app (routing) → widgets → features → entities → shared
   - **React**: app → pages → widgets → features → entities → shared
   - Lower layers cannot import from upper layers

2. **Direct references between different slices in the same layer are prohibited**

   - Cannot directly import features/profile from features/auth
   - If communication between same-layer slices is needed, compose in upper layer

3. **Each slice is accessed only through public API**

   - Export only through each slice's index.ts
   - Do not expose internal implementation

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Export through index.ts
// src/features/auth/index.ts
export { LoginForm } from './ui/LoginForm';
export { useAuth } from './model/useAuth';

// Usage elsewhere
import { LoginForm, useAuth } from '@/src/features/auth';

// ❌ Bad: Direct import
import { LoginForm } from '@/src/features/auth/ui/LoginForm';
```

4. **Next.js Special Rules**
   - Keep `app` directory's page.tsx as thin as possible
   - Separate server components and client components
   - Move all business logic to FSD structure in `src`

```typescript
// ✅ Good: app handles routing only
// app/users/[id]/page.tsx
import { UserProfileWidget } from '@/src/widgets/user-profile';

export default async function UserPage({ params }: { params: { id: string } }) {
  return <UserProfileWidget userId={params.id} />;
}

// ❌ Bad: Business logic in app
// app/users/[id]/page.tsx
export default async function UserPage({ params }: { params: { id: string } }) {
  const user = await fetchUser(params.id); // ❌ Business logic
  return <div>{user.name}</div>; // ❌ UI logic
}
```

### Component Separation Principles

Components should be split as small as possible:

1. **Single Responsibility**: One component performs one clear role
2. **100-line limit**: Review separation if component exceeds 100 lines
3. **Reusability**: Separate logic used 2+ times into its own component
4. **Presentation/Container Pattern**: Consider separating UI and logic

```typescript
// ❌ Bad: One large component
const UserProfile = () => {
  // 200 lines of complex logic and UI
};

// ✅ Good: Split into small components
const UserProfile = () => (
  <div>
    <UserHeader user={user} />
    <UserStats stats={stats} />
    <UserPosts posts={posts} />
    <UserActions onEdit={handleEdit} />
  </div>
);
```

## 2. Component Writing Rules

### Component Structure Order

1. Import statements
2. Type/interface definitions
3. Component function
4. Style definitions (when using styled-components)
5. Export statement

### Example

```typescript
// 1. Import
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { Button } from '@/components/common';

// 2. Type definition
interface UserCardProps {
  name: string;
  email: string;
  onEdit?: () => void;
}

// 3. Component
export const UserCard: React.FC<UserCardProps> = ({ name, email, onEdit }) => {
  const [isHovered, setIsHovered] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Effect logic
  }, []);

  return (
    <div onMouseEnter={() => setIsHovered(true)}>
      <h3>{name}</h3>
      <p>{email}</p>
      {onEdit && <Button onClick={onEdit}>Edit</Button>}
    </div>
  );
};
```

### Component Writing Principles

- **Functional components** (avoid class components)
- **Single Responsibility Principle**: One component, one role
- **Avoid Props Drilling**: Use Context API or state management library
- **Conditional Rendering**: Use ternary operator or && operator
- **List Rendering**: Always use unique key prop

## 3. Naming Conventions

### File Names

- **Components**: PascalCase (e.g., `UserCard.tsx`)
- **Hooks**: camelCase with 'use' prefix (e.g., `useAuth.ts`)
- **Utilities**: camelCase (e.g., `formatDate.ts`)
- **Constants**: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (e.g., `API_ENDPOINTS.ts`)

### Variable and Function Names

- **Components**: PascalCase (e.g., `UserProfile`)
- **Functions/Variables**: camelCase (e.g., `handleSubmit`, `userName`)
- **Constants**: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (e.g., `MAX_RETRY_COUNT`)
- **Private Variables**: underscore prefix (e.g., `_internalState`)
- **Boolean**: is/has/should prefix (e.g., `isLoading`, `hasError`)

### Event Handlers

- Use **handle** prefix (e.g., `handleClick`, `handleSubmit`)
- Use **on** prefix when passing to components (e.g., `onClick`, `onSubmit`)

## 4. TypeScript Usage Rules

### Type Definitions

- **No `any` usage**: Use `unknown` or specific types
- **Interface vs Type**:
  - Prefer interface for object shapes
  - Use type for Union, Intersection
- **Explicit type declarations**: Declare function return types

### Example

```typescript
// ✅ Good
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
}

type UserRole = 'admin' | 'user' | 'guest';

function getUser(id: number): Promise<User> {
  // ...
}

// ❌ Bad
function getUser(id: any) {
  // ...
}
```

### Props Types

```typescript
// ✅ Explicit Props definition
interface ButtonProps {
  label: string;
  onClick: () => void;
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary';
  disabled?: boolean;
}

// When children are present
interface CardProps {
  title: string;
  children: React.ReactNode;
}
```

## 5. Hooks Usage Rules

### Basic Principles

- **Call only at top level**: Prohibited inside conditionals, loops
- **Call only within React functions**: Only in components or custom hooks
- **Custom hooks**: Names starting with 'use'

### useState

```typescript
// ✅ Clear initial value
const [count, setCount] = useState<number>(0);
const [user, setUser] = useState<User | null>(null);

// ✅ Functional update (based on previous state)
setCount((prev) => prev + 1);
```

### useEffect

```typescript
// ✅ Accurately specify dependency array
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData();
}, [userId]); // Execute only when userId changes

// ✅ Use cleanup function
useEffect(() => {
  const subscription = subscribeToData();
  return () => subscription.unsubscribe();
}, []);
```

### Custom Hooks

```typescript
// ✅ Separate reusable logic
function useLocalStorage<T>(key: string, initialValue: T) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState<T>(() => {
    const stored = localStorage.getItem(key);
    return stored ? JSON.parse(stored) : initialValue;
  });

  useEffect(() => {
    localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(value));
  }, [key, value]);

  return [value, setValue] as const;
}
```

## 6. State Management

### Local State vs Global State

- **Local State**: Used in single component → useState
- **Shared State**: Multiple components → Context API or Zustand/Recoil
- **Server State**: Recommended to use React Query / TanStack Query

### Context API Usage

```typescript
// context/AuthContext.tsx
interface AuthContextType {
  user: User | null;
  login: (credentials: Credentials) => Promise<void>;
  logout: () => void;
}

export const AuthContext = createContext<AuthContextType | undefined>(undefined);

export const useAuth = () => {
  const context = useContext(AuthContext);
  if (!context) {
    throw new Error('useAuth must be used within AuthProvider');
  }
  return context;
};
```

## 7. Performance Optimization

### React Compiler Usage

This project uses **React Compiler** for automatic optimization.

```typescript
// ✅ React Compiler automatically optimizes
// No need for memo, useMemo, useCallback

// Write components normally
export const UserList = ({ users }: { users: User[] }) => {
  return (
    <div>
      {users.map((user) => (
        <UserCard key={user.id} user={user} />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
};

// Normal value calculation
const filteredUsers = users.filter((user) => user.isActive);
const totalCount = calculateTotal(items);

// Normal function definition
const handleClick = () => {
  doSomething();
};
```

### Optimization Principles

1. **Leave it to React Compiler**

   - Do not use memo, useMemo, useCallback
   - Compiler automatically optimizes necessary parts

2. **When manual optimization is needed**
   - Consider Web Worker for very complex calculations
   - Use virtualization library for infinite scroll (react-window, @tanstack/react-virtual)

```typescript
// ❌ Bad: Unnecessary memoization
const value = useMemo(() => a + b, [a, b]);
const handleClick = useCallback(() => onClick(), [onClick]);

// ✅ Good: Write naturally
const value = a + b;
const handleClick = () => onClick();
```

### Dynamic Import

```typescript
// Code splitting
const HeavyComponent = dynamic(() => import('./HeavyComponent'), {
  loading: () => <Spinner />,
  ssr: false,
});
```

### Image Optimization

```typescript
// Use Next.js Image component
import Image from 'next/image';

<Image
  src="/photo.jpg"
  alt="Description"
  width={500}
  height={300}
  priority // For LCP images
/>;
```

## 8. Error Handling

### try-catch Usage

```typescript
async function fetchUserData(userId: string) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`);
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error('Unable to fetch user information');
    }
    return await response.json();
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error fetching user:', error);
    throw error; // Or appropriate error handling
  }
}
```

### Error Boundary

```typescript
// components/ErrorBoundary.tsx
class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component<Props, State> {
  static getDerivedStateFromError(error: Error) {
    return { hasError: true, error };
  }

  componentDidCatch(error: Error, errorInfo: ErrorInfo) {
    console.error('Error caught:', error, errorInfo);
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.hasError) {
      return <ErrorFallback error={this.state.error} />;
    }
    return this.props.children;
  }
}
```

## 9. Accessibility (a11y)

### Semantic HTML

```typescript
// ✅ Use semantic tags
<button onClick={handleClick}>Click</button>
<nav><a href="/home">Home</a></nav>

// ❌ Non-semantic
<div onClick={handleClick}>Click</div>
```

### ARIA Attributes

```typescript
<button aria-label="Close menu" aria-expanded={isOpen} onClick={toggleMenu}>
  <MenuIcon />
</button>
```

### Keyboard Navigation

- All interactive elements must be accessible via keyboard
- Tab order should be logical
- Buttons activate with Enter/Space keys

## 10. Design System (Tailwind CSS)

### Basic Principles

This project uses **Tailwind CSS** to implement modern and rich designs.

1. **Modern Design**: Sophisticated UI reflecting latest design trends
2. **Responsive Required**: All components perfectly adapt from mobile to desktop
3. **Rich UI**: Visually rich and informative design rather than simple
4. **Professional and Trustworthy**: Friendly yet not playful
5. **Light Animations**: Natural transition effects that enhance user experience

### Tailwind CSS Usage Rules

#### Prefer Utility Classes

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Use Tailwind utility classes
<div className="flex items-center justify-between p-6 bg-white rounded-lg shadow-md hover:shadow-lg transition-shadow">
  <h2 className="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">Title</h2>
  <button className="px-4 py-2 bg-blue-600 text-white rounded-md hover:bg-blue-700 transition-colors">
    Button
  </button>
</div>

// ❌ Bad: Inline styles
<div style={{ display: 'flex', padding: '24px' }}>
  <h2 style={{ fontSize: '24px', fontWeight: 'bold' }}>Title</h2>
</div>
```

#### Minimize Custom Classes

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Use Tailwind's @apply (only for common patterns)
// globals.css
.btn-primary {
  @apply px-4 py-2 bg-blue-600 text-white rounded-md hover:bg-blue-700 transition-colors;
}

// ❌ Bad: All styles as custom classes
.custom-button {
  padding: 8px 16px;
  background-color: #2563eb;
  color: white;
  /* ... */
}
```

